  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nA mix of high, middle and low clouds are gradually filling\nSouthern California skies this morning. There is a band of light \nshowers over the coastal waters. This band will get a boost of \nenergy from an incoming low-pressure trough, which will enhance \nthe showers and spread them ashore late this morning and inland \nthis afternoon, before heading out of the region late tonight. \nRainfall will range from about 0.10 inch in northern Orange County\nand the northern Inland Empire, up to about 0.70 inch in top \nmountain spots. Light measurable snow is expected above 7,500 feet\nor so. It appears San Diego County will get a greater share of \nrain from this storm. Winds will increase too, mainly over the \ncoastal waters, mountains, and deserts. Widespread gusts in the \nmountains and deserts should exceed 30 mph, with isolated spots \nexceeding 40 mph. As the system exits stage right, drier but not\nwarmer weather will follow Tuesday through Wednesday night. High \ntemperatures will range from 2-12 degrees below seasonal averages,\nwith highs only in the 60s in the coastal basin, lower 70s in\nlower deserts, 50s in the High Desert, and 40s and 50s (yes, for\nhighs) in the mountains. The next system arriving early Thursday \nis driven by a stronger jet so it will have more energy to make \nrain from the moisture and propel stronger winds than today's\nsystem has. Thursday's system tracks a little more to the north, \nso there is some uncertainty in its strength for SoCal's latitude,\nbut we'll gain greater focus on the intensity and impact in the \nnext couple days. For now we are fairly certain it will bring \ngreater rainfall and stronger winds than today's system. \nPreliminary precipitation estimates range from a half inch around \nSan Diego, 1 inch in Orange County, up to 2.5 inches in the San \nBernardino Mountains, one-third to one-half inch in the High \nDesert, and less than one quarter inch in the lower deserts. \nIsolated wind gusts are likely to top 60 mph in the favored wind \ncorridors, 35-55 mph across the rest of the mountains and deserts,\nand 25-30 mph for coast and valleys. Snow levels will come down \nto around 6,500 feet (Big Bear Lake level), and a few inches could\naccumulate above 7,000 feet. Yet another system appears on the \nhorizon, arriving Sunday and/or Monday. This one looks weaker, so \nnot as wet or windy as the Thursday system. Its projected \nlocation, timing, and character is all over the place among \nensemble solutions. The most certain thing we can say is that our \nweather will remain unsettled and cool.",
